<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Task 90-android/photo. Gradle merges this into the app's manifest (Unity
  Manual, "Android App Manifest": plug-in manifests are one of the sources
  merged into the final Android App Manifest).

  NOT ONE <uses-permission>. That is the whole design, and it is the reason
  the system photo picker was chosen over ACTION_PICK:

    - MediaStore.ACTION_PICK_IMAGES runs in the MediaProvider's own process
      and hands back a URI the caller has already been granted read access
      to: "the caller gets read access to user picked items even without
      storage permissions"
      (developer.android.com/reference/android/provider/MediaStore
      #ACTION_PICK_IMAGES). The guide page describes the picker at length but
      does not contain that sentence; the reference page does, so the
      reference page is what is cited.
      With the floor at 33 the picker is simply there, so this is the only
      gallery path: no ACTION_GET_CONTENT fallback, no Play-services
      backport, no availability shim.
    - ACTION_PICK would need READ_MEDIA_IMAGES (READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE below
      33) to read what it hands back. That is the one permission this design
      exists to avoid, and the reason it is not here is not "we might need
      it" - it is that nothing in this plug-in ever touches a MediaStore row.
    - ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE delegates to the camera app, which holds the
      camera permission itself. The caller needs android.permission.CAMERA
      only if it DECLARES it: "Note: if you app targets M and above and
      declares as using the CAMERA permission which is not granted, then
      attempting to use this action will result in a SecurityException."
      (developer.android.com/reference/android/provider/MediaStore
      #ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE - quoted with its own typo, "if you app").
      Declaring it here would therefore add a runtime prompt and buy nothing,
      so it is not declared.

  That keeps 60-shell-build/17-permission-audit's standard: every permission
  justified or removed - here, removed by construction.

  <queries> is not a permission. It is Android 11 package visibility
  (developer.android.com/training/package-visibility): without it
  resolveActivity() returns null for intents this app does not handle itself,
  which would hide the camera button on every device and send every gallery
  pick down the fallback path. startActivity is not filtered, but the "is this
  possible at all?" checks are, and this plug-in makes three of them.

  The proxy Activity exists because onActivityResult belongs to
  UnityPlayerGameActivity, which is Unity's and not ours to override. A
  translucent, no-title activity that launches the real picker and forwards
  the result is the standard shape for a Unity Android plug-in that needs a
  result back. android:exported="false" because nothing outside the app has
  any business starting it.

  ${applicationId} is an Android Gradle Plugin manifest placeholder; AGP
  substitutes the application's real id when it merges a library manifest.
  CatPicker.java rebuilds the same authority at runtime from
  getPackageName(). If those two ever disagree, getUriForFile throws
  IllegalArgumentException rather than handing the camera the wrong place to
  write.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <queries>
        <!-- Is there a camera app to delegate to? -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.media.action.IMAGE_CAPTURE" />
        </intent>
        <!-- The system photo picker. Guaranteed present at minSdk 33; this
             only lets resolveActivity confirm it before an intent is fired,
             so a stripped Android image declines cleanly instead of throwing
             ActivityNotFoundException. The <data> has to mirror the
             setType("image/*") on the intent itself, or the filter does not
             match. There is no GET_CONTENT entry: with the floor at 33 there
             is no fallback path left to declare. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.provider.action.PICK_IMAGES" />
            <data android:mimeType="image/*" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application>
        <activity
            android:name="com.catshelter.picker.CatPickActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|keyboardHidden|uiMode|density" />

        <provider
            android:name="com.catshelter.picker.CatPickFileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.catpick"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/catpick_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>

</manifest>
